T-05 — DNS Failure Isolation (Runbook A)
Captured: 2026-11-24 · Method: guided lab run in the deployed browser simulator
Environment: production GitHub Pages build (lab/index.html), Chromium 158, desktop viewport
URL: https://peterken-365.github.io/-Automated-SME-Helpdesk-Access-Governance-Dual-OS-Endpoint-Hardening-Architecture/lab/
Scope note: verifies the control LOGIC and decision paths; real-endpoint screenshots remain for the physical lab session.
State mutation: step 1 sets S.dnsBroken=true — the free-play terminal's nslookup also fails until step 5 resets it (cross-view consistency verified).

=== step 1: Run DNS break ===
C:\Users\awanjiru> Set-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet 2" -ServerAddresses 192.168.56.53
DNS servers set to 192.168.56.53 — a server that does not exist.

[lab state changed: free-terminal nslookup now fails too]

=== step 2: Run gateway ping ===
C:\Users\awanjiru> ping 192.168.56.1
Reply from 192.168.56.1: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128
Reply from 192.168.56.1: bytes=32 time<1ms TTL=128

PASS — physical/link/IP layers healthy.

=== step 3: Run external ping ===
C:\Users\awanjiru> ping 8.8.8.8
Reply from 8.8.8.8: bytes=32 time=48ms TTL=113
Reply from 8.8.8.8: bytes=32 time=47ms TTL=113

PASS — routing is fine. The problem is not the network.

=== step 4: Run nslookup ===
C:\Users\awanjiru> nslookup google.com
DNS request timed out.
    timeout was 2 seconds.
*** Request to 192.168.56.53 timed-out

VERDICT: IP passes, name fails → DNS-layer failure (Runbook A isolation complete).

=== step 5: Run DNS reset + re-verify ===
C:\Users\awanjiru> Set-DnsClientServerAddress -InterfaceAlias "Ethernet 2" -ResetServerAddresses
DNS servers reset.
C:\Users\awanjiru> nslookup google.com
Name:    google.com
Address:  142.250.183.14

Service restored — and the root-cause prevention note goes in the ticket.

